l="nofollow">Twitter Web App</a>', 'note_tweet': {'is_expandable': True, 'note_tweet_results': {'result': {'id': 'Tm90ZVR3ZWV0OjE4ODA5NzY4OTQ1ODcyMzIyNTY=', 'text': '🧵 "Unrestricted Warfare: China\'s Master Plan To Destroy America" is a book written by two Chinese colonels, Qiao Liang and Wang Xiangsui, published in 1999. Here\'s a summary along with its key points and principles, along with a link to the PDF. \n\nAdditionally, I\'ve outlined some parallels between today\'s current events and Unrestricted Warfare:\n\nSummary:\n"Unrestricted Warfare" argues that traditional military tactics are becoming obsolete in the face of modern technology and global interconnectedness. The authors suggest that future conflicts will transcend conventional warfare, employing non-military means to achieve strategic goals. They describe warfare as encompassing all aspects of society where any means necessary can be used to subdue an enemy, including economic, political, cyber, and psychological warfare.\n\nKey Points and Principles:\nBroad Definition of Warfare:\nWarfare is not just military action; it includes economic, cultural, and informational battles.\nAsymmetric Warfare:\nStrong nations can be fought by weaker ones through unconventional methods that exploit vulnerabilities in areas like finance, media, or technology.\nNon-Traditional Tactics:\nTactics like cyber warfare, financial manipulation, and the use of international law or media can be weaponized.\nCombination of Methods:\nIntegrating various forms of warfare (military, psychological, technological, etc.) to overwhelm the opponent.\nExploitation of Globalization:\nUse of global economic interdependence and international laws to one\'s advantage.\nSuperiority Through Non-Confrontational Means:\nAvoiding direct military engagement but achieving victory through other forms of warfare.\nLegal Warfare (Lawfare):\nUsing legal systems to tie up or discredit opponents.\nParallels with Current Events in the US:\nCyber Attacks: The increase in cyberattacks on US infrastructure and private companies can be seen as a form of unrestricted warfare, where adversaries use digital means to disrupt services without direct military confrontation.\nEconomic Warfare:\nEconomic policies, trade wars, or currency manipulations (like those seen in US-China trade relations) reflect the principle of using economic tools as weapons.\nInformation and Psychological Operations:\nThe spread of disinformation, influencing elections, or manipulating public opinion through social media can be viewed through the lens of unrestricted warfare. Events like the 2016 US election interference allegations illustrate this.\nLegal and Diplomatic Battles:\nThe use of international bodies or legal frameworks to challenge or restrict US policies or actions, such as disputes at the World Trade Organization or International Court of Justice.\nCultural and Ideological Warfare:\nEfforts to influence or undermine cultural values or national identity through media, education, or technology companies.\nTechnological Dominance:\nThe race for technological supremacy, especially in AI, 5G, and quantum computing, can be interpreted as a non-direct form of warfare where control over technology standards and intellectual property becomes a battleground.\n\nThese parallels suggest that the concepts in "Unrestricted Warfare" are not just theoretical but are being practiced in various forms today. This is particularly relevant as nations, corporations, and even non-state actors engage in global competition that transcends traditional military engagements. However, it\'s important to analyze these events critically, understanding that not all international actions are necessarily warfare, but might be part of complex international relations where economic, cultural, and technological dominance plays a significant role.', 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': []}, 'richtext': {'richtext_tags': []}, 'media': {'inline_media': []}}}}, 'legacy': {'bookmark_count': 1, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Sun Jan 19 13:53:33 +0000 2025', 'conversation_id_str': '1880976894922752018', 'display_text_range': [0, 275], 'entities': {'hashtags': [], 'media': [{'display_url': 'pic.x.com/xSmBQvdp2I', 'expanded_url': 'https://x.com/lmaobrbttyl/status/1880976894922752018/photo/1', 'id_str': '1880973797769007104', 'indices': [276, 299], 'media_key': '3_1880973797769007104', 'media_url_https': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/media/GhqPtAfW0AAybWA.jpg', 'type': 'photo', 'url': 'https://t.co/xSmBQvdp2I', 'ext_media_availability': {'status': 'Available'}, 'features': {'large': {'faces': [{'x': 518, 'y': 313, 'h': 278, 'w': 278}]}, 'medium': {'faces': [{'x': 414, 'y': 250, 'h': 222, 'w': 222}]}, 'small': {'faces': [{'x': 234, 'y': 141, 'h': 126, 'w': 126}]}, 'orig': {'faces': [{'x': 518, 'y': 313, 'h': 278, 'w': 278}]}}, 'sizes': {'large': {'h': 1500, 'w': 1003, 'resize': 'fit'}, 'medium': {'h': 1200, 'w': 802, 'resize': 'fit'}, 'small': {'h': 680, 'w': 455, 'resize': 'fit'}, 'thumb': {'h': 150, 'w': 150, 'resize': 'crop'}}, 'original_info': {'height': 1500, 'width': 1003, 'focus_rects': [{'x': 0, 'y': 938, 'w': 1003, 'h': 562}, {'x': 0, 'y': 497, 'w': 1003, 'h': 1003}, {'x': 0, 'y': 357, 'w': 1003, 'h': 1143}, {'x': 37, 'y': 0, 'w': 750, 'h': 1500}, {'x': 0, 'y': 0, 'w': 1003, 'h': 1500}]}, 'allow_download_status': {'allow_download': True}, 'media_results': {'result': {'media_key': '3_1880973797769007104'}}}, {'display_url': 'pic.x.com/xSmBQvdp2I', 'expanded_url': 'https://x.com/lmaobrbttyl/status/1880976894922752018/photo/1', 'id_str': '1880973875367780352', 'indices': [276, 299], 'media_key': '3_1880973875367780352', 'media_url_https': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/media/GhqPxhkWIAA-OUg.jpg', 'type': 'photo', 'url': 'https://t.co/xSmBQvdp2I', 'ext_media_availability': {'status': 'Available'}, 'features': {'large': {'faces': []}, 'medium': {'faces': []}, 'small': {'faces': []}, 'orig': {'faces': []}}, 's